1B bond comfortably (n5.d2). Norvane's 2027 capex is planned up 12%, mostly substations and transformers (n2.d4), and the market looks able to fund that.\n- **Pellham's problem is equity, not debt.** Pellham withdrew its C$2.4B equity offering after demand fell short (n5.d3). It is cutting 2027 transformer orders by about 40% and deferring two substation programmes \"until financing improves.\" Its 2027 capex plan was up 18%. I found no evidence on whether Pellham has tried to borrow, so I can't say whether debt could replace the withdrawn equity.\n- **Equity is weak across the sector.** Two utility share offerings were cut back in size (n5.d2). That limits how much debt the utilities can add without straining their credit.\n\n**Conclusion:** Debt markets can probably finance Norvane's plan. For Pellham, the largest single buyer of grid equipment in Caldria, the evidence suggests the plan will be cut back, because a debt-only substitute for the equity looks unproven. For anyone asking about grid-equipment demand, the Pellham cuts matter more than the debt market's openness.\n\n**What would change this view:** a Pellham bond deal, or a statement on its leverage and ratings headroom. Wider spreads in Q4 would also weaken the case.","ttft_ms":3672,"type":"result","duration_ms":21349,"uuid":"fe617b4d-bd94-4717-a550-269d01027a9c","ttft_stream_ms":2736,"time_to_request_ms":224,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":15,"system_prompt":6,"resume_other":0,"process_user_input":9,"input_attachments":33,"input_hooks":4,"conversation_append":0,"transcript_persist":40,"tool_pool_refresh":0,"init_message":19,"init_frame_send":3,"engine_pickup":2,"loop_entry":4,"message_prep":0,"model_config_wait":3,"autocompact":2,"request_announcements":21,"query_setup":0,"model_gates":17,"tool_schema_build":12,"message_normalization":8,"client_creation":6,"request_build":20},"turn_start_resume_kind":"none","process_turn_index":0,"time_to_request_loop_lag_ms":113,"time_to_request_cpu_ms":91,"time_t